course: dictionary
lesson: 1375
title: Capable: able, and good at things
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Capable has two meanings. First, it means able to do something: a swimmer might be capable of swimming a length of the pool. Second, it means good at things, skilled and sensible. «A capable pupil» is someone who does their work well.
explain: Look at the words around capable. «Capable of» is a clue: it is followed by something a person or thing can do, like a car capable of 100 miles an hour. Ask yourself: able to do what? That tells you which meaning is in use.
explain: Capable is the opposite of incapable, which means unable. In «I'm capable of tying my own shoes!» the speaker is proud and sure they can do it without help.
explain: Adults use capable in school reports. Reports rarely say a child is lazy. Instead they may say «capable of more», which means the child could do better than they are doing now. You have to read between the lines to understand it.
fact: Capable means able to do something, or good at things, skilled and sensible.
fact: «Capable of» is followed by what someone or something can do, as in «capable of swimming a length».
fact: «A capable pupil» means a pupil who does their work well.
fact: «Capable of more» means the pupil could do better than they are doing now.
fact: The opposite of capable is incapable, which means unable.
exercise: Maya is «capable of swimming a length». What does capable mean here? | Sleepy after lunch | Afraid of water | *Able to do something | Cold and wet
exercise: A teacher writes, «a capable pupil». What does this suggest? | *A pupil who does their work well | A pupil who is ill | A pupil who is always late | A pupil who is new to the school
exercise: What is the opposite of capable? | Clever | *Incapable | Careless
test: A report says «capable of more». What does this mean about the pupil? | *The pupil could do better than they are doing now | The pupil has finished all their work | The pupil is already doing their very best | The pupil is ill and must go home
test: In «I'm capable of tying my own shoes!», what does the speaker mean? | They tie shoes for other people | They have lost their shoes | *They can tie their own shoes | They are not allowed to tie shoes
exam: Which word is a synonym for able? | *Capable | Incapable | Sleepy | Careless
